                "Differentiation shifts the iterated derivation by one.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()),
            Node("BalaFlow.flowMap", "flowMap", Formula3(),
                "Choose the ring homomorphism with the derivation flow as its underlying function.", DescribeRole.Definition, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()),
            Node("BalaFlow.constantCoeff_flow", "constantCoeff flow", Formula4(),
                "The constant coefficient is evaluation of the initial observable.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()),
            Node("BalaFlow.flow_of_d_eq_zero", "flow of d eq zero", Formula5(),
                "An invariant of the derivation gives a constant flow.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()),
            Node("BalaCRT.prime_power_periods_to_all_moduli", "prime power periods to all moduli", Formula6(),
                "Prime-power congruences combine into the specified period for every positive modulus, since totients respect divisibility.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()),
            Node("claim", "claim", Formula7(),
                "a(n) = (2*n-1)! * [x^(2*n-1)] S(x), where S(x) = Integral (1 + S(x)^2)^2 dx. Conjecture: the sequence {a(n)} reduced modulo a positive integer k is eventually periodic with the period dividing phi(k)^2, where phi(n) = A000010(n). An example is given below. - _Peter Bala_, Feb 18 2026 The source is unpaginated. The primitive has constant coefficient zero, eCoeff is factorial times the ordinary coefficient, and all sequences take integer values. Natural subtraction is truncated subtraction.", DescribeRole.Definition, AssessedProvenance.FromLiterature(Source)),
            Node("result", "result", Formula8(),
                "The integral derivation flow supplies the series and its integer exponential coefficients. Prime-power operator annihilators give the prescribed shift, and divisibility assembles all positive moduli.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo(Source)),
            Node("BalaFlow.existsFlowMap", "existsFlowMap", Formula9(),
                "The iterated Leibniz rule makes the derivation flow multiplicative, and its zero, one and addition laws complete the ring homomorphism.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()),
            Node("BalaFlow.flowMapApply", "flowMapApply", Formula10(),
                "The chosen homomorphism evaluates to the derivation flow at every observable.", DescribeRole.Theorem, AssessedProvenance.FromRepo()))));

